It is half past eight in the morning and the colonnade is almost empty. The spa guests have finished their first cup and gone to their treatments; the next wave will not arrive until eleven. In between there is an hour and a half in which a hundred and twenty metres of cast-iron arcade belong to a single couple — and to a photographer who knows this light will not come round again.

This article is not a how-to; for that there is the [planning guide](/en/magazine/wedding-in-marienbad) and the [overview of venues, halls and capacities](/en/weddings). This is a walk through the places as they look — in the order a wedding day usually takes them.

## The Main Colonnade

The cast-iron structure of 1889 was cast in Blansko and bolted together on site like a kit. The ceiling is painted, the floor polished to a mirror, and between the columns the light falls in regular stripes — which is why people photograph here even when the wedding itself is somewhere else.

{% figure
   src="/images/library/wedding/main-colonnade-arcade-wide.jpg"
   alt="View down the Main Colonnade arcade with the wedding couple at its centre"
   caption="The run of the arcade pulls the eye all the way to the end — and the train on the polished floor does the rest."
   width="wide" /%}

The colonnade is public space; nobody will close it off for you alone. That sounds like a drawback, but mostly it means the morning hour is free here and nobody moves you along. In the afternoon it reverses: the promenade comes alive, people walk between the columns with drinking cups, and the pictures take on an entirely different mood — livelier, less composed.

{% gallery columns=2 caption="Morning under the vault: the arcade just after eight, and the first dance in the same place twelve hours later." %}
{% gallery-image src="/images/library/wedding/main-colonnade-couple-arcade.jpg" alt="Wedding couple in the Main Colonnade arcade with its painted ceiling" /%}
{% gallery-image src="/images/library/wedding/main-colonnade-couple-dancing.jpg" alt="Newlyweds dancing beneath the arches of the Main Colonnade in Mariánské Lázně" /%}
{% /gallery %}

How the colonnade came about and why it looks the way it does is covered in [the article on its history](/en/magazine/history-of-the-colonnade).

## The Singing Fountain

The circular fountain lies in front of the colonnade and plays on every odd hour. The repertoire is fixed, a piece runs about ten minutes, and after dark the lights join in. For a wedding this has one practical quality: it is the only item on the programme you can plan to the minute without having to book it.

{% figure
   src="/images/library/wedding/cross-spring-pavilion-bouquet.jpg"
   alt="Wedding couple with the bridal bouquet before the colonnade columns"
   caption="The colonnade columns by the fountain are the shortest route from ceremony to first pictures — a few dozen metres."
   width="wide" /%}

{% pullquote
   text="The whole wedding day fits into a ten-minute walk here."
   cite="From the ceremony to the banquet and on to the hotel" /%}

A special piece at a time of your choosing can be arranged, but the terms are set by whoever operates the fountain and it needs notice. More on how the fountain came about and what it plays is in [a separate article](/en/magazine/singing-fountain).

## The Cross Spring pavilion and the park

A few hundred metres from the colonnade stands a white pavilion with a copper dome. Lawn in front of it, forest behind — and between them enough room for a small ceremony, after which you walk to the banquet.

{% gallery columns=3 caption="Three views of the same place: the lawn in front of the pavilion, the colonnade columns, and a portrait between them." %}
{% gallery-image src="/images/library/wedding/cross-spring-pavilion-couple-walking.jpg" alt="Wedding couple in front of the Cross Spring pavilion in the spa park" /%}
{% gallery-image src="/images/library/wedding/cross-spring-pavilion-couple.jpg" alt="Bride and groom by the white Cross Spring colonnade" /%}
{% gallery-image src="/images/library/wedding/cross-spring-colonnade-columns.jpg" alt="Newlyweds among the white columns of the spa colonnade" /%}
{% /gallery %}

White columns have the property of throwing light back into faces. Photographically that means soft shadows even at midday, when the sun is unusable anywhere else — which is why people come here at precisely the hour nobody else would choose.

## The end of the colonnade and the fresco

The colonnade ends in a flight of steps beneath a ceiling fresco. It is the darkest corner of the whole walkway and at the same time the only place where the background tells a story of its own rather than simply being architecture.

{% figure
   src="/images/library/wedding/main-colonnade-fresco-steps.jpg"
   alt="Newlyweds on the steps beneath the fresco at the end of the Main Colonnade"
   caption="The steps under the fresco close off the colonnade — and are often the last frame before the banquet."
   width="wide" /%}

{% figure
   src="/images/library/wedding/main-colonnade-fresco-portrait.jpg"
   alt="Portrait of the wedding couple in front of the Main Colonnade fresco"
   caption="The dark ground of the fresco works as a natural portrait studio."
   width="wide" /%}

## Indoors: the Marble Hall

In town, the banquet most often takes place in the Casino social house. The Marble Hall has a frescoed ceiling, crystal chandeliers and a parquet floor that has been danced on since 1900 — and it is the only one that fits a wedding you do not have to trim.

{% figure
   src="/images/library/wedding/casino-marble-hall-banquet.jpg"
   alt="The Marble Hall of the Casino social house set for a wedding banquet"
   caption="The hall laid for the banquet, an hour before the first guests arrive."
   width="full" /%}

{% gallery columns=3 caption="The laid table up close: the seating, the flowers and the sashes on the chairs." %}
{% gallery-image src="/images/library/wedding/casino-marble-hall-tables.jpg" alt="Wedding tables in the historic hall with its frescoed ceiling" /%}
{% gallery-image src="/images/library/wedding/banquet-place-setting-detail.jpg" alt="Close-up of a place setting with name card at the wedding table" /%}
{% gallery-image src="/images/library/wedding/casino-hall-floral-centrepiece.jpg" alt="Tall floral centrepiece on the wedding table in the historic hall" /%}
{% /gallery %}

Capacities for each hall — from fifty in Edward's Library to three hundred seated in the Marble Hall — are [in the table on the weddings page](/en/weddings#saly).

## Outdoors and in the green

Not every ceremony happens in town. When it is to be in the open, you look for a meadow, a pavilion or the edge of a wood — and the mood is entirely different: less architecture, more grass and trees.

{% gallery columns=3 caption="An open-air ceremony: the pavilion, the first kiss, and the rings straight afterwards." %}
{% gallery-image src="/images/library/wedding/garden-pavilion-couple.jpg" alt="Wedding couple in a garden pavilion" /%}
{% gallery-image src="/images/library/wedding/outdoor-ceremony-first-kiss.jpg" alt="The couple's first kiss at an outdoor ceremony" /%}
{% gallery-image src="/images/library/wedding/wedding-rings-detail.jpg" alt="Close-up of the newlyweds' hands with their wedding rings" /%}
{% /gallery %}

An outdoor ceremony is always planned with a hall in reserve, though. In a basin at six hundred and thirty metres it rains in June too, and the decision is made on the morning itself — which is why it pays to design the flowers so they can be carried indoors.

